<<<DOC 12>>>
Title: Q3 Suite Refresh — What Actually Moved

We ran our dead weight census before the sprint and it came back uglier than last time — almost a third of the legacy suite is now either universal-pass or universal-fail across every version we've fielded since March. The dead weight census doesn't lie about this kind of thing; once an item stops separating your best model from your worst it's just burning compute for a number nobody trusts.

Triaging the fail cluster turned up something else: three "new" failures were actually groove lock. The agent had just finished a run of file-move tasks and kept trying to relocate files on a task that wanted them edited in place instead. Cleared session history, reran, and the groove lock disappeared — same weights, same prompt, nothing wrong with the model itself.

Cross-checked the timing against the rig diary and neither dip lines up with last week's timeout bump, so that's a separate ticket. Kept the rig diary open in a side window most of the week and it already saved us one false alarm.

Patched some of the census gap with synthetic ladders generated overnight. The first synthetic ladders batch turned out too easy for the mid tier, so we queued a second round with harder seeds. Ran a salt run on both batches before trusting any of it, randomizing run ids and filenames. Scores held steady under randomization, so the salt run cleared them for the live suite.

<<<DOC 13>>>
Title: Self-Authored Items — First Read

Tried the ouroboros items approach this cycle since our dead weight census keeps flagging holes faster than we can hand-author replacements. The dead weight census specifically called out the mid-difficulty tier, so that's where we pointed the ouroboros items experiment first.

Had the frontier agent draft two hundred candidates and ran a second model as filter to strip anything it judged trivially easy for the author. Ouroboros items are a decent stopgap for volume but I wouldn't trust them yet as the sole source for a capability claim — the filter missed easy-for-author items more than once, and the batch still skewed soft on the task family the author agent is best at.

Ran twin runs on the survivors before publishing, same task with plain-text phrasing against the version with our usual boilerplate formatting. The twin runs gap came out around six points on the ouroboros batch versus two points on the hand-authored control, which reads as presentation cost, not capability, and it's bigger here than expected.

Also caught a groove lock mid-scoring: the agent had just finished a string of retrieval-then-summarize tasks and kept summarizing when the new item wanted a direct answer instead. Reset the session and the groove lock cleared on the next pass.

Ran a salt run afterward, randomizing file names and timestamps across the whole batch. The salt run came back clean, scores didn't move, so at least the harness isn't leaking metadata into this one.

<<<DOC 14>>>
Title: The Rig Diary Earned Its Keep

Score dropped four points Tuesday and the first instinct was to blame the model update, but the rig diary showed we'd bumped the tool-call timeout that same afternoon, which lined up better than the model swap did. Kept the rig diary open in a side window the rest of the week and it caught two more false alarms before Friday, one traced to a retry-count change and one to a prompt-template edit nobody had mentioned in standup.

Separately, we're leaning harder on synthetic ladders for the mid-difficulty band since hand-authoring there has the worst throughput of any tier we run. Queued a fresh synthetic ladders batch overnight and it filled about half the gap our last refresh flagged.

Mixed in a small set of ouroboros items too, generated by last quarter's checkpoint instead of the current one, specifically to dodge the difficulty-skew problem you get when the author and the evaluated agent are the same model. The ouroboros items held up better than I expected once we checked them against the control set — no obvious softness toward the older checkpoint's habits.

Before merging any of it we ran twin runs across the new items, wording-only variants of the same twenty tasks. The twin runs spread was tighter on the synthetic ladders batch than on our hand-authored baseline, which is a decent sign the phrasing isn't doing the discriminating work instead of the task content.
